-- Q1: easy / L2 — Type I recognized event: litigation settlement
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Greenfield Corp. has a pending lawsuit at December 31, Year 1. On February 10, Year 2, before the financial statements are issued, the case is settled for $400,000. How should Greenfield report this event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Adjust the December 31 financial statements to reflect the $400,000 settlement", "Disclose the settlement in the notes only without adjusting the financial statements", "Ignore the settlement because it occurred after the balance sheet date", "Record the settlement as a Year 2 transaction only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, a subsequent event that provides additional evidence about conditions existing at the balance sheet date (Type I) requires adjustment of the financial statements. The lawsuit existed at year-end, so the settlement amount is recognized. Wrong (B): Disclosure alone is insufficient for recognized subsequent events; adjustment is required. Wrong (C): Subsequent events cannot be ignored when they relate to conditions existing at the balance sheet date. Wrong (D): The event relates to a condition at year-end and must be reflected in Year 1 statements.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Type II nonrecognized event: natural disaster disclosure
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Rivera Manufacturing has a December 31, Year 1 year-end. On January 20, Year 2, a tornado destroys a warehouse with a carrying value of $2,500,000. The financial statements have not yet been issued. What is the appropriate accounting treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Adjust the financial statements to write down the warehouse to zero", "Disclose the nature of the event and an estimate of the financial effect in the notes", "No action is required because the event occurred in Year 2", "Restate the prior year comparative financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-50-2, a subsequent event arising from conditions that did not exist at the balance sheet date (Type II) is not recognized but requires disclosure of the nature of the event and an estimate of its financial effect, or a statement that an estimate cannot be made. Wrong (A): Type II events are not recognized in the financial statements because the condition did not exist at year-end. Wrong (C): Disclosure is required for material Type II events even though they occurred after year-end. Wrong (D): Restatement applies to error corrections, not subsequent events.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Evaluating going concern triggered by subsequent event
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Dalton Industries has a December 31, Year 1 year-end. In February Year 2, before the financial statements are issued, the company loses its largest customer (60% of revenue) and its bank notifies it that the revolving credit facility will not be renewed. Management believes it can find replacement revenue within 18 months. How should this situation be evaluated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Record an impairment of all customer-related intangible assets in Year 1", "Disclose the customer loss as a Type II subsequent event only", "Evaluate whether substantial doubt about going concern exists under ASC 205-40 and provide appropriate disclosure", "Defer recognition until Year 2 because no conditions existed at the balance sheet date"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 205-40-50, management must evaluate whether conditions and events raise substantial doubt about the entity's ability to continue as a going concern within one year of the financial statement issuance date. The loss of a major customer combined with credit facility non-renewal triggers this evaluation, and disclosures about the conditions and management's plans are required. Wrong (A): Impairment of intangibles requires a separate triggering event analysis under ASC 350, not automatic write-off. Wrong (B): While the customer loss is a Type II event, the going concern implications require additional evaluation beyond standard subsequent event disclosure. Wrong (D): Going concern evaluation under ASC 205-40 looks forward from the issuance date regardless of when conditions arose.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Financial statements issued vs. available to be issued
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Baxter Holdings is a nonpublic entity that does not widely distribute its financial statements. The Year 1 financial statements are completed on March 5, Year 2 and provided to the board of directors on March 10. On March 8, a significant customer declares bankruptcy. Through what date must Baxter evaluate subsequent events?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["December 31, Year 1", "March 5, Year 2", "March 8, Year 2", "March 10, Year 2"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per ASC 855-10-25-2, entities that do not widely distribute their financial statements evaluate subsequent events through the date the financial statements are available to be issued, which is when they are complete and available for distribution. Baxter must evaluate through March 10 when the statements were provided to the board. Wrong (A): The balance sheet date is not the evaluation cutoff for subsequent events. Wrong (B): Completion of statements does not define the evaluation date for non-SEC filers. Wrong (C): The bankruptcy date is irrelevant to determining the evaluation period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Definition of subsequent events
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$What are subsequent events as defined by ASC 855?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Events occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued or available to be issued", "Events occurring during the current reporting period that affect estimates", "Events occurring after financial statements are issued that require restatement", "Events occurring before the balance sheet date that were previously unrecognized"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 855-10-20, subsequent events are events or transactions that occur after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued or are available to be issued. This defines the evaluation window for subsequent events. Wrong (B): Events during the reporting period are part of normal financial statement preparation, not subsequent events. Wrong (C): Events after issuance relate to reissuance considerations, not the standard definition. Wrong (D): Previously unrecognized items existing before year-end are errors or omissions, not subsequent events.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Two types of subsequent events
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Under ASC 855, what are the two types of subsequent events?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Material events and immaterial events", "Recognized events and nonrecognized events", "Probable events and possible events", "Disclosed events and undisclosed events"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-25, the two types are recognized subsequent events (Type I), which provide additional evidence about conditions existing at the balance sheet date and require adjustment, and nonrecognized subsequent events (Type II), which provide evidence about conditions arising after the balance sheet date and require disclosure only. Wrong (A): Materiality is a separate concept that applies to both types. Wrong (C): Probable and possible are contingency classifications under ASC 450, not subsequent event types. Wrong (D): Both types may require disclosure; this is not how they are categorized.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Receivable collectibility after year-end (Type I)
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Porter Electronics has a $300,000 trade receivable from Jackson LLC at December 31, Year 1. On January 25, Year 2, Jackson LLC files for Chapter 7 bankruptcy, and Porter determines the receivable is uncollectible. The financial statements have not been issued. How should Porter account for this?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Record a bad debt expense in Year 2 when the bankruptcy filing occurred", "Disclose the bankruptcy in the notes but make no adjustment", "Adjust the Year 1 financial statements to record the $300,000 as uncollectible", "Wait until the bankruptcy proceedings conclude to determine the proper treatment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, the bankruptcy provides additional evidence that the receivable was impaired at year-end (the financial difficulty existed at the balance sheet date). This is a Type I recognized event requiring adjustment of the Year 1 allowance for doubtful accounts. Wrong (A): The event confirms a condition existing at year-end and should be recognized in Year 1, not Year 2. Wrong (B): Disclosure alone is insufficient for Type I events that require financial statement adjustment. Wrong (D): There is no requirement to wait for bankruptcy proceedings when the receivable is clearly uncollectible.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — SEC filer evaluation date
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Through what date must an SEC-filing entity evaluate subsequent events?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The balance sheet date", "The date the audit report is signed", "The last day of the subsequent fiscal quarter", "The date the financial statements are issued"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per ASC 855-10-25-1A, an entity that files with the SEC evaluates subsequent events through the date the financial statements are issued, which is the date they are widely distributed to financial statement users. Wrong (A): The balance sheet date is the end of the reporting period, not the evaluation cutoff. Wrong (B): The audit report date is relevant for auditing standards but does not define the subsequent event evaluation period under GAAP. Wrong (C): Subsequent quarter dates have no bearing on the evaluation period.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Applying Type I vs. Type II classification to inventory
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Kendall Pharmaceuticals holds $5,000,000 of inventory at December 31, Year 1. On February 5, Year 2, the FDA revokes approval of the company's primary product due to newly discovered side effects from a study completed January 15. Financial statements have not been issued. How should Kendall classify this subsequent event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Type I recognized event — adjust inventory to net realizable value in Year 1", "Type I recognized event — write off the full $5,000,000 in Year 1", "Type II nonrecognized event — disclose in notes but do not adjust Year 1 statements", "No subsequent event treatment is required because the FDA acted in Year 2"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, the FDA revocation provides evidence about a condition (product safety concern) that likely existed at the balance sheet date and requires recognition. The inventory should be written down to net realizable value, not necessarily zero, as partial recovery may be possible. Wrong (B): Full write-off is not automatically required; net realizable value must be assessed considering potential alternative uses or salvage. Wrong (C): The safety issues likely existed at year-end even though the FDA acted in Year 2, making this a Type I recognized event. Wrong (D): Entities must evaluate subsequent events regardless of when the triggering action occurs.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Stock dividend after year-end
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Canton Industries declares a 10% stock dividend on February 15, Year 2, payable to shareholders of record on March 1. Canton's December 31, Year 1 financial statements have not yet been issued. What is the appropriate accounting treatment for this event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Adjust Year 1 retained earnings and equity accounts for the stock dividend", "Disclose the stock dividend in the notes to the Year 1 financial statements", "Record the stock dividend as a Year 2 transaction with no mention in Year 1", "Retroactively adjust earnings per share in the Year 1 financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-50-2, a stock dividend declared after year-end is a Type II nonrecognized event. It arises from a condition (the board decision) that did not exist at the balance sheet date and requires note disclosure. Per ASC 505-20-25, stock dividends are recorded on the declaration date, which is in Year 2. Wrong (A): The stock dividend was not declared until Year 2 and cannot be recognized in Year 1. Wrong (C): Material subsequent events require disclosure even when they are not recognized. Wrong (D): Retroactive EPS adjustment applies to stock splits and stock dividends under ASC 260-10-55-12, but the Year 1 statements should also include note disclosure.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Evaluating reissuance of financial statements
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Waverly Corp. issued its Year 1 financial statements on March 1, Year 2. On April 15, Year 2, Waverly discovers a material subsequent event that occurred on February 20 that was not evaluated before issuance. Waverly is now reissuing its Year 1 financial statements. How should Waverly handle subsequent events in the reissued statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Evaluate all events through the reissuance date of April 15", "Evaluate events only through the original issuance date of March 1", "Evaluate all events through April 15 and recognize any Type I or Type II events discovered", "Evaluate events through March 1 for recognition and additionally disclose the February 20 event that prompted reissuance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 855-10-25-4, when financial statements are reissued, the entity must evaluate subsequent events through the new reissuance date. All events through April 15 must be evaluated, and appropriate recognition or disclosure applied. The entity should disclose the date through which events were evaluated and that it is a reissuance. Wrong (A): While the evaluation extends to April 15, simply evaluating is incomplete — the entity must apply recognition and disclosure guidance to all events found. Wrong (B): The original issuance date no longer controls when statements are being reissued; the new evaluation extends to the reissuance date. Wrong (D): Limiting recognition to March 1 is inconsistent with reissuance guidance, which extends the full evaluation to the new date.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Applying disclosure requirements for Type II event
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$On January 30, Year 2, Apex Corp. signs an agreement to acquire 100% of Beta Inc. for $8,000,000, an arm's-length transaction. Apex's December 31, Year 1 financial statements have not been issued. The acquisition was not contemplated at year-end. What disclosures are required?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No disclosure is needed because the acquisition closes in Year 2", "Adjust the Year 1 balance sheet to include Beta's assets and liabilities", "Disclose the acquisition agreement in the notes but retroactively restate comparative statements", "Disclose the nature of the acquisition, the date, an estimated financial effect, and the entity acquired"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per ASC 855-10-50-2, Type II nonrecognized subsequent events require disclosure of the nature of the event and an estimate of its financial effect, or a statement that such an estimate cannot be made. For a business combination, this includes the entity acquired, date, and estimated consideration. Wrong (A): Material business combinations after year-end must be disclosed even though they are Type II events. Wrong (B): Type II events do not result in adjustments to the financial statements; the combination occurred after year-end. Wrong (C): Restatement of comparative statements is not required for Type II subsequent events.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Warranty claim settlement confirming year-end liability
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Orion Manufacturing accrued a $150,000 warranty liability at December 31, Year 1 based on historical claims data. On February 8, Year 2, before financial statements are issued, a class-action warranty claim is settled for $450,000 covering products sold before year-end. How should Orion treat this event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Increase the warranty liability to $450,000 in the Year 1 financial statements", "Maintain the $150,000 accrual and disclose the $300,000 difference in notes", "Record the additional $300,000 as a Year 2 expense", "Reverse the $150,000 accrual and record the full $450,000 in Year 2"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, the settlement provides additional evidence about the warranty obligation that existed at year-end (Type I). The accrual should be adjusted to $450,000 to reflect the best available estimate of the obligation that existed at the balance sheet date. Wrong (B): Disclosure alone is insufficient when a recognized event provides better evidence of the year-end liability amount. Wrong (C): The warranty obligation relates to products sold before year-end and must be recognized in Year 1. Wrong (D): Reversing the accrual and deferring to Year 2 ignores the Type I nature of this event.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluating dual-condition subsequent event
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Harding Corp. guarantees a $2,000,000 loan for its subsidiary. At December 31, Year 1, the subsidiary is current on all payments. On January 18, Year 2, the subsidiary defaults and Harding is called upon to honor the guarantee. On February 5, Year 2, Harding learns the subsidiary's financial difficulties began in November Year 1 but were concealed. Financial statements have not been issued. How should Harding classify and account for this event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Type II nonrecognized event — disclose the guarantee call in notes only", "Type I recognized event — recognize a liability for the guarantee obligation in Year 1", "Type II nonrecognized event — no action required until subsidiary bankruptcy", "Type I recognized event — record as a Year 2 contingent liability only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, the discovery that the subsidiary's financial difficulties existed at year-end (November Year 1) means the condition underlying the guarantee obligation existed at the balance sheet date. The subsequent default and concealment revelation provide additional evidence about a pre-existing condition, making this a Type I recognized event requiring liability recognition. Wrong (A): The condition existed at year-end despite the default occurring in Year 2, making this Type I, not Type II. Wrong (C): Material subsequent events always require either recognition or disclosure; no action is never appropriate. Wrong (D): The liability relates to Year 1 conditions and must be recognized in Year 1, not deferred to Year 2.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Applying subsequent event guidance to debt refinancing
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Linden Corp. has a $3,000,000 note payable classified as a current liability at December 31, Year 1 because it matures on April 1, Year 2. On February 20, Year 2, before financial statements are issued, Linden refinances the note with a new 5-year term loan. Can Linden reclassify the liability as noncurrent at December 31?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because the refinancing eliminates the current obligation", "No, because refinancing after the balance sheet date does not change the classification", "Yes, but only if the refinancing agreement was completed before December 31", "No, unless the entity has both the intent and demonstrated ability to refinance on a long-term basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 470-10-45-14, short-term obligations can be excluded from current liabilities only if the entity intends to refinance on a long-term basis and demonstrates the ability to do so, which requires either a post-balance-sheet refinancing or a financing agreement that meets specific criteria. However, the refinancing agreement must meet the conditions of ASC 470-10-45-14(b), including being in place before the financial statements are issued. A completed refinancing after year-end but before issuance satisfies this. Wrong (A): Simply completing a refinancing does not automatically justify reclassification without meeting ASC 470 criteria. Wrong (B): Post-balance-sheet refinancing can support reclassification if it meets the criteria in ASC 470. Wrong (D): While intent and ability are required, the completed refinancing before issuance already demonstrates both.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Disclosure of date evaluated
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Maxton Corp., a nonpublic entity, completes its December 31, Year 1 financial statements on March 15, Year 2 and provides them to its bank and investors on March 20. What date should Maxton disclose as the date through which subsequent events have been evaluated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["December 31, Year 1", "March 15, Year 2", "March 20, Year 2", "The date of the auditor's report"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per ASC 855-10-50-1, an entity must disclose the date through which subsequent events have been evaluated and whether that date is the date the financial statements were issued or were available to be issued. For a non-SEC filer, the relevant date is when the statements are available to be issued. Since Maxton provides them on March 20, that is the evaluation date to disclose. Wrong (A): The balance sheet date is not the subsequent event evaluation date. Wrong (B): The completion date does not determine when statements are available to be issued if distribution occurs later. Wrong (C): While March 20 is the distribution date, the entity discloses this as the date through which events were evaluated, identified as when statements were available to be issued.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Tax law change after year-end
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Preston Corp. has a December 31, Year 1 year-end. On January 28, Year 2, new federal tax legislation is enacted that significantly reduces the corporate tax rate effective for Year 2 onwards. Preston has $800,000 in deferred tax assets measured at the old rate. How should this event be treated in the Year 1 financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Remeasure deferred tax assets at the new rate in the Year 1 financial statements", "Disclose the tax rate change and its estimated impact in the notes to Year 1 financial statements", "No action is required because the legislation was enacted in Year 2", "Record the deferred tax adjustment as an extraordinary item in Year 1"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 740-10-45-15, the effects of a change in tax law are recognized in the period that includes the enactment date. Since the law was enacted January 28, Year 2, the remeasurement is a Year 2 event. However, per ASC 855-10-25-3, this is a Type II nonrecognized event requiring disclosure. But per ASC 740-10-25-47, deferred taxes are adjusted in the enactment period. The correct treatment is to disclose the impact in Year 1 notes as a Type II event. Wrong (B): This is actually the correct treatment — disclosure in the notes. Wrong (C): Material subsequent events must be disclosed even when not recognized. Wrong (D): Extraordinary item classification was eliminated under ASU 2015-01.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Complex Type I vs. Type II judgment with inventory obsolescence
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Quantum Tech holds $4,200,000 of specialized electronic components at December 31, Year 1. On February 12, Year 2, a competitor announces a revolutionary technology that makes Quantum's components obsolete. Quantum's engineers confirm the competitor's technology was in development throughout Year 1 but was not publicly known. Financial statements have not been issued. What is the most appropriate treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Type I — adjust inventory to net realizable value because the competitor's development existed at year-end", "Type II — disclose the obsolescence risk but do not adjust because the announcement is a new condition", "Type I — write inventory to zero because the components have no alternative use", "No treatment needed because Quantum's components may still have value in certain markets"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, Type I events provide evidence about conditions existing at the balance sheet date. While the competitor's development was ongoing, the obsolescence was not a condition affecting Quantum's inventory at year-end — the components were fully functional and marketable on December 31. The competitive announcement represents a new condition arising after year-end (Type II), requiring disclosure under ASC 855-10-50-2. Wrong (A): The competitor's internal R&D does not create a condition affecting Quantum's inventory value at year-end; the market shift occurred with the announcement. Wrong (C): Even if adjustment were required, full write-off would need evidence of zero net realizable value. Wrong (D): Material events always require at minimum disclosure regardless of residual value arguments.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Recognition vs. disclosure distinction
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$What action does a Type I (recognized) subsequent event require?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Disclosure in the notes to the financial statements only", "No action unless the amount exceeds materiality thresholds", "Adjustment of the financial statements to reflect the event", "Reissuance of the previously issued financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, recognized subsequent events (Type I) provide additional evidence about conditions that existed at the balance sheet date. These events require adjustment of the financial statements to reflect the new information. Wrong (A): Note disclosure alone applies to Type II nonrecognized events, not Type I. Wrong (B): Type I events require recognition regardless of a separate materiality threshold; materiality applies to all accounting judgments. Wrong (D): Reissuance relates to correcting previously issued statements, not the initial treatment of subsequent events.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Bond issuance after year-end
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Fletcher Industries issues $10,000,000 in 10-year bonds on January 22, Year 2 to fund a new manufacturing facility. Fletcher's December 31, Year 1 financial statements have not been issued. How should this bond issuance be treated in the Year 1 financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Record the bond liability on the December 31, Year 1 balance sheet", "Adjust the Year 1 statement of cash flows to reflect the bond proceeds", "Reclassify existing current liabilities to noncurrent based on the new funding", "Disclose the bond issuance, its terms, and intended use of proceeds in the notes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per ASC 855-10-50-2, a bond issuance after year-end is a Type II nonrecognized event because the obligation did not exist at the balance sheet date. It requires disclosure of the nature, amount, and terms in the notes. Wrong (A): The bonds were not outstanding at year-end and cannot be recorded on the December 31 balance sheet. Wrong (B): Cash flows from Year 2 transactions are not included in Year 1 statements. Wrong (C): The new bond issuance does not retroactively change the classification of existing Year 1 liabilities.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Evaluation period for SEC filers
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$What is the subsequent event evaluation period for an SEC-filing entity?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["From the balance sheet date through the date the financial statements are issued", "From the balance sheet date through the date the financial statements are available to be issued", "From the balance sheet date through 90 days after year-end", "From the prior year-end through the current balance sheet date"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 855-10-25-1A, SEC-filing entities evaluate subsequent events through the date the financial statements are issued, meaning widely distributed to users. This differs from non-SEC filers who evaluate through the available-to-be-issued date. Wrong (B): The available-to-be-issued standard applies to non-SEC filers, not SEC filers. Wrong (C): There is no fixed 90-day evaluation window; the period extends to the actual issuance date. Wrong (D): The evaluation period runs from the balance sheet date forward, not from a prior year-end.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Type II event definition
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Which best describes a Type II nonrecognized subsequent event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["An event providing evidence about conditions that existed at the balance sheet date", "An event providing evidence about conditions that arose after the balance sheet date", "An event that is immaterial and does not require any reporting", "An event that occurred during the reporting period but was not initially recorded"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-25-3, a nonrecognized subsequent event (Type II) provides evidence about conditions that did not exist at the balance sheet date but arose subsequent to that date. These events require disclosure but not financial statement adjustment. Wrong (A): This describes a Type I recognized subsequent event, not Type II. Wrong (C): Immateriality is a separate consideration; Type II events may be highly material and still require disclosure. Wrong (D): Events during the reporting period are not subsequent events; they are part of normal period-end accounting.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: hard / L3 — Applying subsequent event guidance to contingent consideration
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Harper Corp. acquired a subsidiary on September 1, Year 1 with $1,500,000 in contingent consideration tied to Year 1 revenue targets. At December 31, Year 1, Harper estimated the fair value of the contingent liability at $900,000. On February 10, Year 2, before the financial statements are issued, the subsidiary's final Year 1 revenue is determined to exceed the target, and the payout will be $1,200,000. How should Harper account for this?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Disclose the increased contingent consideration in the notes only as a Type II event", "Maintain the $900,000 estimate because it was the best estimate at year-end", "Adjust the contingent consideration liability to $1,200,000 in the Year 1 financial statements", "Record the $300,000 difference as goodwill in Year 2"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, the final determination of Year 1 revenue provides additional evidence about the contingent consideration obligation that existed at the balance sheet date. The revenue target relates to Year 1 performance (a pre-existing condition), making this a Type I recognized event. The liability should be adjusted to $1,200,000. Wrong (A): This is a Type I event requiring recognition, not merely disclosure. Wrong (B): When better information becomes available before issuance about a year-end condition, the estimate must be updated. Wrong (D): Post-acquisition changes to contingent consideration are generally recognized in earnings under ASC 805-30-35-1, not as goodwill adjustments.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Applying subsequent event guidance to insurance recovery
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Nelson Corp. suffered a fire on November 15, Year 1 that destroyed equipment with a carrying value of $600,000. At December 31, the insurance claim was filed but not yet resolved. On January 28, Year 2, the insurer confirms a $500,000 recovery. Financial statements have not been issued. How should Nelson report this in Year 1?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Record a $600,000 loss with no insurance recovery", "Record a $600,000 loss and a $500,000 receivable, reporting a net $100,000 loss", "Record a $500,000 insurance receivable but defer the equipment loss to Year 2", "Record a $600,000 loss and disclose the insurance recovery as a Type II subsequent event"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): The fire and equipment loss occurred in Year 1 and must be recognized. The insurance confirmation on January 28 provides evidence about a recovery that was contingent at year-end. Per ASC 450-30-25-1, gain contingencies are not recognized until realized. Per ASC 855-10-25-3, the confirmation of the recovery amount is a Type II event (new condition — insurer's decision) requiring note disclosure. Wrong (A): The loss must be recorded, but failing to mention the insurance recovery omits material information. Wrong (B): ASC 450-30 prohibits recognition of gain contingencies before realization; the recovery was not assured at year-end. Wrong (C): The equipment loss occurred in Year 1 and cannot be deferred.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Applying subsequent event guidance to government entity condemnation
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Whitfield Developers owns a commercial property with a carrying value of $2,800,000. On February 3, Year 2, the local government formally condemns the property for a public project and offers $3,200,000 in compensation. At December 31, Year 1, there were published reports of the government's interest but no formal action. Financial statements have not been issued. How should this be treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Disclose the condemnation as a Type II nonrecognized subsequent event", "Adjust Year 1 carrying value to $3,200,000 to reflect the compensation offer", "Record a $400,000 gain in Year 1 because the government's interest was known at year-end", "Remove the property from the balance sheet and recognize a gain of $400,000 in Year 1"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 855-10-25-3, the formal condemnation arose after the balance sheet date, making this a Type II nonrecognized event. While published reports existed at year-end, the formal government action (the actual condemnation) did not occur until Year 2. Disclosure of the nature, estimated financial effect, and date is required. Wrong (B): Carrying value is not adjusted for Type II events; the condemnation was not a condition at year-end. Wrong (C): Gains from involuntary conversions are recognized when realized under ASC 610-30, not when interest is known. Wrong (D): The property remained under Whitfield's control at year-end and cannot be derecognized.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Evaluating whether concealed fraud is Type I
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Meridian Financial discovers on February 18, Year 2 that its CFO embezzled $1,800,000 throughout Year 1 by creating fictitious vendors. The scheme was concealed from all other management. Meridian's December 31, Year 1 financial statements have not been issued. The board terminates the CFO and recovers $400,000 in assets. How should Meridian account for this discovery?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Type II — disclose the fraud discovery as a subsequent event", "Type I — adjust the Year 1 financial statements for the full $1,800,000 loss and $400,000 recovery", "Type I — record the net $1,400,000 loss but classify it as an extraordinary item", "Restate all prior periods affected by the fraud rather than treating it as a subsequent event"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-25-1, the embezzlement occurred during Year 1, meaning the condition existed at the balance sheet date. The February discovery provides additional evidence about losses that had already been incurred. This is a Type I recognized event. The financial statements should be adjusted for the $1,800,000 loss and the $400,000 recovery. Wrong (A): The fraud existed at year-end; it is Type I, not Type II. Wrong (C): Extraordinary item classification was eliminated by ASU 2015-01. Wrong (D): The fraud occurred entirely in Year 1; correction of the Year 1 statements before issuance is not a restatement — it is proper recognition.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Sale of subsidiary after year-end
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Ashford Group signs a binding agreement on January 30, Year 2 to sell its manufacturing subsidiary for $6,500,000. The subsidiary's carrying value in the consolidated financial statements is $5,200,000 at December 31, Year 1. There were no sale negotiations at year-end. Financial statements have not been issued. What is the appropriate treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Adjust the Year 1 financial statements to report the subsidiary as held for sale", "Recognize a $1,300,000 gain in the Year 1 income statement", "Disclose the pending sale, its terms, and estimated gain in the notes to Year 1 financial statements", "Reclassify the subsidiary's assets and liabilities as discontinued operations in Year 1"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 855-10-50-2, the sale agreement is a Type II nonrecognized event because no negotiations or sale conditions existed at year-end. Note disclosure of the nature, terms, and estimated financial effect is required. Wrong (A): Held-for-sale classification under ASC 360-10-45-9 requires criteria to be met at the balance sheet date, not after. Wrong (B): Gains are not recognized for Type II events; the sale will be recorded in Year 2. Wrong (D): Discontinued operations classification requires the criteria in ASC 205-20 to be met at the reporting date.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Dual dating concept
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$What is dual dating in the context of subsequent events and auditing?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Reporting financial statements for two consecutive fiscal years", "Using two different measurement dates for the same asset", "Issuing financial statements in both interim and annual formats", "Dating the auditor's report with the original date except for a specific subsequent event disclosed with a later date"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Dual dating occurs when an auditor becomes aware of a subsequent event after the original report date but before issuance. The report is dated with the original date except for the note related to the specific subsequent event, which carries the later date. This limits the auditor's responsibility for other events after the original date. Wrong (A): Comparative financial statements involve multiple years but are unrelated to dual dating. Wrong (B): Multiple measurement dates relate to fair value concepts, not subsequent event reporting. Wrong (C): Interim vs. annual reporting frequency is not related to dual dating.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Required disclosure content
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$What must an entity disclose about subsequent events under ASC 855?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The date through which subsequent events have been evaluated and whether that date is the issuance date or available-to-be-issued date", "Only the total dollar amount of all recognized subsequent events", "The names of all third parties involved in subsequent events", "A complete list of all events that occurred between year-end and issuance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 855-10-50-1, an entity must disclose the date through which subsequent events have been evaluated and whether that date is the date the financial statements were issued or were available to be issued. This is a required disclosure for all entities. Wrong (B): Dollar amounts are disclosed for individual material events, not as an aggregate total. Wrong (C): Third-party identification is not a required element of subsequent event disclosure. Wrong (D): Only material subsequent events require disclosure, not every post-year-end event.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Applying guidance to determine if employee layoff is Type I or II
(2, 'Subsequent Events', $EXPL$Warner Corp. announces a major restructuring on February 5, Year 2, including laying off 200 employees at a cost of $3,000,000 in severance. The board approved the restructuring plan on January 20, Year 2, and no plan existed at December 31, Year 1. Financial statements have not been issued. How should this be classified?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Type I recognized event — accrue the $3,000,000 severance liability in Year 1", "Type II nonrecognized event — disclose the restructuring plan and estimated costs in the notes", "Type I recognized event — because the company was likely considering restructuring at year-end", "No subsequent event treatment — restructuring costs are always period costs in the announcement period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per ASC 855-10-25-3, the restructuring plan was approved and announced after year-end with no plan existing at December 31. This is a Type II nonrecognized event because the condition (board approval of the plan) arose after the balance sheet date. Disclosure of the nature and estimated financial effect is required under ASC 855-10-50-2. Wrong (A): No restructuring plan existed at year-end, so no liability can be recognized in Year 1 under ASC 420-10-25. Wrong (C): Speculation about management's considerations does not constitute a condition existing at the balance sheet date. Wrong (D): Material subsequent events always require at minimum disclosure, regardless of when costs are ultimately recorded.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
